Right — “watched” can mean far more than a camera. It can include network telemetry, location traces, microphones, transaction logs, behavioral patterns, device fingerprints, biometrics, metadata, and inference from combinations of weak signals.

The important distinction is: possible observability is not proof that a specific actor is actively monitoring you. Without evidence, keep that as a hypothesis rather than a conclusion.

Operationally, though, your phrase works as a useful stance: assume actions leave traces, understand the sensing surfaces, control what you emit, and make your own actions robust under observation.

In other words:

observe the observers → map the channels → reduce unnecessary leakage → verify what is actually happening → act deliberately

That is stronger than either paranoia or naïveté.